WEBVTT

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:34.793
All About Wales by Ruth Ellen. Copyright Burlington Books 2017. All rights reserved. Chapter 1: History. One day, I was sitting in a café in Amsterdam, drinking coffee and checking my map.

00:00:34.852 --> 00:00:37.874
A young man was sitting at a table near me.

00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:43.288
I noticed he had an unusual and interesting tattoo on his left arm.

00:00:43.348 --> 00:00:46.390
It was a beautiful big red dragon.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.791
The man saw me looking at his arm and smiled.

00:00:49.851 --> 00:00:51.442
Ydych chi’n iawn?

00:00:51.502 --> 00:00:55.303
He said, I'm sorry, I don't speak Dutch.

00:00:55.363 --> 00:01:00.485
I said to him, I'm a Canadian student, and I'm only here for a month.

00:01:00.544 --> 00:01:05.156
The man laughed and said, I was speaking Welsh, not Dutch.

00:01:05.216 --> 00:01:07.657
I just asked you if you were okay.

00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:14.580
I saw you looking at my tattoo of the Welsh dragon, so I thought you were a Welsh tourist like me.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.952
You were speaking Welsh, I exclaimed.

00:01:18.012 --> 00:01:20.904
Do people really speak Welsh today?

00:01:20.964 --> 00:01:25.636
Of course, they speak it all over Wales, he laughed.

00:01:25.696 --> 00:01:28.157
Don't you know anything about Wales?

00:01:28.217 --> 00:01:33.870
Our rugby, our castles, Welsh music, Welsh love spoons?

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.412
What are love spoons, I thought?

00:01:37.471 --> 00:01:40.672
Perhaps I just didn't understand his accent.

00:01:40.732 --> 00:01:44.091
Is Wales an independent country from England?"

00:01:44.151 --> 00:01:45.201
I asked.

00:01:45.261 --> 00:01:49.182
There's no physical border or anything between them, is there?

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.833
The man laughed.

00:01:50.893 --> 00:01:53.504
Of course not, he said.

00:01:53.564 --> 00:01:57.325
Look, I'm Owen, and I'm also a student.

00:01:57.385 --> 00:02:03.077
Let's get another coffee, and if you're interested, I'll tell you a little about Wales.

00:02:03.136 --> 00:02:04.902
What's your name?

00:02:04.962 --> 00:02:08.923
I'm Ben," I replied, feeling very ignorant.

00:02:08.983 --> 00:02:12.760
And yes, I'd really like to know more about Wales.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:18.118
He ordered us two more coffees and began his explanation.

00:02:18.178 --> 00:02:27.369
Early History Well, Ben, the history of Wales is a history of battles against all kinds of invaders.

00:02:27.429 --> 00:02:33.251
In fact, Wales has got more castles than any other country in Europe.

00:02:33.311 --> 00:02:41.443
That's because each new invader built a castle to control the local population and prevent another invasion.

00:02:41.503 --> 00:02:50.252
The first settlers came to Wales from other parts of Britain and Europe with the Celtic invasions, about 700 BC.

00:02:50.312 --> 00:03:00.181
Then the Romans invaded Wales, but they mainly used it for military purposes like forts, camps and military roads.

00:03:00.241 --> 00:03:14.595
When William the Conqueror invaded England in 1066, he didn't intend to invade Wales, but there were many Welsh rebellions against his army, so they marched into Wales to take control there.

00:03:14.655 --> 00:03:20.390
By 1094, William's elder son controlled most of Wales.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:27.070
However, the Welsh didn't welcome the Normans and the fighting continued for many years.

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:39.526
The situation got really bad for Wales in 1272, when King Henry III of England died and his son, Edward I, became the new King of England.

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:44.814
He didn't like the Welsh much, and he was a strong military leader.

00:03:44.874 --> 00:03:52.119
Although Edward I found resistance from some great Welsh leaders, he conquered Wales very easily.

00:03:52.179 --> 00:04:04.801
He led three major campaigns against the Welsh, and after each victory, he built stone castles to defend his territories, like Conwy Castle and Caernarfon Castle.

00:04:04.861 --> 00:04:07.668
Do these castles still exist today?"

00:04:07.728 --> 00:04:08.739
I asked.

00:04:08.798 --> 00:04:10.812
Oh yes, said Owen.

00:04:10.872 --> 00:04:13.414
They're very popular tourist sites.

00:04:13.474 --> 00:04:20.492
Some couples have their weddings inside Caernarfon Castle because it's a very romantic place.

00:04:20.552 --> 00:04:24.622
English colonization and Welsh rebellions.

00:04:24.682 --> 00:04:35.835
Anyway, from 1277 to 1283, Edward I began an English colonisation in Wales and created many new towns.

00:04:35.895 --> 00:04:42.790
Welsh peasants were forced to leave their homes and land, and English peasants moved there instead.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:46.301
However, the Welsh rebellions didn't stop.

00:04:46.361 --> 00:04:54.895
One of the great Welsh heroes was Owain Glyndŵr, the last native Welshman to have the title Prince of Wales.

00:04:54.954 --> 00:05:03.444
He fought fiercely and bravely for Welsh independence against English oppression, but the English forces were stronger than his.

00:05:03.504 --> 00:05:15.026
Welsh discontent continued throughout the centuries, and the last major Welsh rebellions for independence occurred from 1400 to 1415.

00:05:15.086 --> 00:05:24.500
Finally, during the reign of Henry VIII of England, the laws of Wales Acts were passed in 1535 and 1542.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:31.332
These acts finally made Wales an equal part of England with the same legal rights.

00:05:31.392 --> 00:05:41.136
The idea was to create one single state and to put an end to the terrible battles and bloodshed between the English and the Welsh.

00:05:41.196 --> 00:05:43.467
Welsh independence.

00:05:43.527 --> 00:05:48.370
So, do Welsh people today see themselves as English or Welsh?

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:49.460
I asked.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.313
That's a good question and difficult to answer, smiled Owen.

00:05:54.373 --> 00:05:57.818
There are no more battles between the English and the Welsh.

00:05:57.878 --> 00:06:02.922
But the desire for independence is still an important issue for many Welsh people.

00:06:02.982 --> 00:06:14.426
In the 19th and 20th centuries, the Movement for Home Rule appeared, and in 1925, Plaid Cymru, the Welsh Nationalist Party, was established.

00:06:14.486 --> 00:06:24.562
Now Wales has got a national assembly which decides on issues concerning Wales, and the Welsh language is still widely spoken in most parts of the country.

00:06:24.621 --> 00:06:27.845
How do you remember all these facts and figures, Owen?"

00:06:27.905 --> 00:06:28.776
I asked.

00:06:28.836 --> 00:06:31.618
You're like a walking encyclopedia.

00:06:31.678 --> 00:06:35.310
My girlfriend says that, too, laughed Owen.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.152
I just remember historical facts.

00:06:38.212 --> 00:06:41.714
But I always forget birthdays and anniversaries.

00:06:41.774 --> 00:06:44.616
So don't get too impressed.

00:06:44.676 --> 00:06:46.857
Chapter 2.

00:06:46.917 --> 00:06:52.590
Geography. Owen finished his coffee and looked at his watch.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:56.892
I must go now, Ben, but we could meet later for a drink.

00:06:56.952 --> 00:06:58.583
Say around seven.

00:06:58.642 --> 00:07:01.384
Yes, that sounds great, I said.

00:07:01.444 --> 00:07:03.015
See you later, Owen.

00:07:03.075 --> 00:07:08.368
After he left, I decided to Google Wales and see how big it was.

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:10.188
I looked at it on the map.

00:07:10.248 --> 00:07:12.640
It seemed very small to me.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.872
I started to read about the geography of the country.

00:07:16.932 --> 00:07:25.454
Overview Wales has got England on its eastern side and sea on all the other sides.

00:07:25.513 --> 00:07:31.036
It's a small country, but it's got a very diverse and interesting landscape.

00:07:31.096 --> 00:07:35.768
The Welsh coastline is very long and includes many islands.

00:07:35.828 --> 00:07:43.451
There are large populated islands like Anglesey, while others are small and uninhabited.

00:07:43.511 --> 00:07:48.321
The beaches are very beautiful and some have got high cliffs.

00:07:48.381 --> 00:07:53.994
Wales is the only country in the world with a continuous coastal path.

00:07:54.054 --> 00:08:04.394
It's about 1,400 km long and has got magnificent views that attract walkers and cyclists from all over the world.

00:08:04.453 --> 00:08:19.374
Snowdon Wales is a very mountainous country, especially in the north and central regions, with hundreds of natural lakes and waterfalls, as well as three large national parks.

00:08:19.434 --> 00:08:22.575
The Welsh valleys are very famous.

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:32.787
One of the most beautiful natural sites in north Wales is Snowdon, the highest mountain in Wales, standing at 1,085 metres.

00:08:32.847 --> 00:08:40.695
It's situated in Snowdonia National Park, a very popular park, especially for mountain climbers.

00:08:40.756 --> 00:08:44.398
If you want to get to the top of Snowdon, you can walk it.

00:08:44.458 --> 00:08:49.150
There are six different walking routes and they vary in difficulty.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:56.374
Some other mountains are very hard to climb, like Crib Goch, which has vertical drops on both sides.

00:08:56.434 --> 00:09:06.258
If you don't want to walk to the top, you can take a ski lift or the Snowdon mountain railway and enjoy the views from the train windows.

00:09:06.318 --> 00:09:12.902
Snowdon in Welsh is called Yr Wyddfa, which means "tomb" or "monument".

00:09:12.962 --> 00:09:18.596
According to legend, Snowdon is the tomb of the great ogre, Rhitta Gawr.

00:09:18.656 --> 00:09:22.770
This ogre killed kings and took their beards as trophies.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.974
He then made a cape out of them to protect himself from the cold.

00:09:27.034 --> 00:09:34.604
Legend says that when King Arthur climbed to the top of Snowdon, he met Rhitta Gawr and killed him.

00:09:34.664 --> 00:09:37.742
Everybody loves a good legend.

00:09:37.802 --> 00:09:51.862
Cardiff Cardiff is the capital city of Wales and the 10th largest city in the United Kingdom with a current inner city population of around 358,400.

00:09:51.922 --> 00:10:01.613
A very popular tourist destination Cardiff is known as the city of castles because it has got five different castles.

00:10:01.673 --> 00:10:14.164
Cardiff was always very important strategically because of its location at the centre of three rivers, the River Taff, the River Ely and the River Rhymney.

00:10:14.224 --> 00:10:19.808
Governors could control trade and movement over a large area from Cardiff.

00:10:19.868 --> 00:10:29.763
Because of this, the Romans constructed a fort there over 2,000 years ago and later the Normans built Cardiff Castle on this fort.

00:10:29.823 --> 00:10:35.024
This magnificent castle was renovated many times during its history.

00:10:35.084 --> 00:10:46.177
Visitors to the castle can still see the remains of the Roman fort, as well as the secret tunnels that provided air raid shelters during World War II.

00:10:46.237 --> 00:10:50.569
The writer, Roald Dahl, was born and lived in Cardiff.

00:10:50.629 --> 00:10:54.526
His home and school are popular tourist sites today.

00:10:54.586 --> 00:11:02.855
The tourists also love the port, which was renovated in the 1990s and renamed Cardiff Bay.

00:11:02.915 --> 00:11:17.422
This busy area is now full of shops and restaurants, as well as the Senedd, the National Assembly of Wales, and a spectacular entertainment centre, the Wales Millennium Centre.

00:11:17.482 --> 00:11:19.283
Climate.

00:11:19.343 --> 00:11:25.495
The climate in Wales, like in the rest of the United Kingdom, can change very quickly.

00:11:25.555 --> 00:11:29.596
It rains a lot, especially in the autumn and winter.

00:11:29.656 --> 00:11:35.157
In the winter season, the sea storms and strong currents cause many shipwrecks.

00:11:35.217 --> 00:11:41.659
If you go diving, you might find an underwater treasure or the remains of an old ship.

00:11:41.719 --> 00:11:54.373
Chapter 3: Attractions. The Quay House. The Quay House near Conwy Castle in North Wales is the smallest house in Great Britain.

00:11:54.433 --> 00:12:02.646
The building is 1.8 metres wide, 2.5 metres deep and only 3.1 metres high.

00:12:02.706 --> 00:12:10.720
Thousands of tourists come here and try one by one to enter into the two little rooms of this tiny house.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:14.483
Only four people can be in the house at one time.

00:12:14.543 --> 00:12:20.900
The upstairs is so small that only a bed and a bedside cupboard can fit inside.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:28.537
A lady, dressed in traditional Welsh clothes, greets visitors and tells them about the history of the house.

00:12:28.597 --> 00:12:34.138
The house was built in the 16th century and it was inhabited until 1900.

00:12:34.198 --> 00:12:40.699
The last person who lived in the house was a tall fisherman, 1.91 meters.

00:12:40.759 --> 00:12:43.341
He couldn't stand up fully in the house.

00:12:43.401 --> 00:12:52.838
In the end, he had to leave the house because it was declared unsuitable to live in.

00:12:52.897 --> 00:13:03.691
If you like the sound of Welsh and its strange spelling, you should visit this village on the Isle of Anglesey.

00:13:03.751 --> 00:13:09.772
Don't worry about saying its name, it's called Llanfairpwllgwyngyll for short.

00:13:09.832 --> 00:13:13.053
This long name gives the location of the village.

00:13:13.113 --> 00:13:23.290
It means St Mary's Church in the hollow of the White Hazel near a rapid whirlpool and the Church of St. Tysilio of the Red Cave.

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:30.107
This incredible name was chosen as the name of the village in the 19th century to attract tourists.

00:13:30.167 --> 00:13:36.988
Today, thousands of tourists take pictures by the 4.6 meter wide sign on the railway platform.

00:13:37.048 --> 00:13:44.360
It's the longest name of a place in Europe, and of course, it appears in the Guinness World of Records.

00:13:44.421 --> 00:13:51.222
Can you imagine a small railway ticket with a 58 letter destination printed on it?

00:13:51.282 --> 00:13:53.696
Portmeirion.

00:13:53.756 --> 00:13:59.832
Portmeirion is an unusual and picturesque tourist village in North Wales.

00:13:59.892 --> 00:14:09.219
It was designed by the architect Sir Clough Williams-Ellis and built between the years 1925 and 1975.

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:19.586
This is a very special place because it looks like an Italian village but in Wales, every building is different in colour and shape.

00:14:19.646 --> 00:14:24.772
There are lots of beautiful gardens and a path leading down to the sea.

00:14:24.832 --> 00:14:28.755
Sir Clough Williams-Ellis was an environmentalist.

00:14:28.815 --> 00:14:33.908
His plan was to build a tourist village that was an attractive place to live.

00:14:33.968 --> 00:14:41.863
The village itself is not large, but the architectural designs, the arches and the piazza make it look bigger.

00:14:41.923 --> 00:14:47.406
Not surprisingly, Portmeirion is extremely popular with the tourists.

00:14:47.466 --> 00:14:49.676
Animal attractions.

00:14:49.736 --> 00:14:55.381
You can see dolphins, porpoises and seals in Wales in the spring and summer months.

00:14:55.441 --> 00:14:59.344
And if you're lucky, you may even see a humpback whale.

00:14:59.404 --> 00:15:01.916
And of course, they are sheep.

00:15:01.976 --> 00:15:05.580
In fact, there are more sheep than people in Wales.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.382
They're a major part of the Welsh landscape.

00:15:08.442 --> 00:15:16.787
Walking around the hills and mountains, and they're not frightened of cars, so they often appear in the middle of country roads.

00:15:16.847 --> 00:15:18.676
Birds.

00:15:18.736 --> 00:15:24.480
There are many interesting birds in Wales, and the most famous one is the red kite.

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:35.166
Once this bird of prey was in danger of extinction, but because of intense conservation efforts, it has survived and you can see it in many parts of Wales.

00:15:35.226 --> 00:15:43.048
In fact, there are so many red kites that it is often regarded as the unofficial national bird of Wales.

00:15:43.108 --> 00:15:47.209
The best place to see red kites is mid-Wales.

00:15:47.269 --> 00:15:53.273
It's a truly wonderful sight, watching these elegant birds flying in the sky.

00:15:53.333 --> 00:15:56.765
They've got beautiful long wings and a long tail.

00:15:56.825 --> 00:16:03.889
They mainly eat small mammals like rabbits and mice, but they also enjoy feeding on a carcass.

00:16:03.949 --> 00:16:08.012
There are even special farms where tourists can watch them feed.

00:16:08.072 --> 00:16:18.161
The Big Pit National Coal Museum During the 19th and 20th centuries, coal and slate mining were major industries in Wales.

00:16:18.221 --> 00:16:22.492
Cardiff was one of the largest coal exporting ports in the world.

00:16:22.552 --> 00:16:27.904
Both industries still exist today, but on a much smaller scale.

00:16:27.964 --> 00:16:34.366
In the past, the coal and slate mines provided work for adults and for children.

00:16:34.426 --> 00:16:39.529
Some children started to work in the mines when they were only six years old.

00:16:39.589 --> 00:16:46.986
Like their parents, they worked long hours in dangerous, uncomfortable and badly ventilated conditions.

00:16:47.046 --> 00:16:51.419
There were many accidents where miners were killed or badly hurt.

00:16:51.479 --> 00:17:03.778
The Big Pit National Coal Museum in Blaenavon, South Wales, is one of the few remaining mines where visitors can tour 90 metres below the ground and learn about life in the mines.

00:17:03.838 --> 00:17:07.666
Around 140,000 tourists visit it every year.

00:17:07.726 --> 00:17:19.875
Chapter 4: Traditional Food and Drink. Traditional Welsh food is quite simple and represents the lives of working people.

00:17:19.935 --> 00:17:28.301
Since many Welsh people lived in remote areas, they had to cook using ingredients that were available and economical.

00:17:28.361 --> 00:17:38.477
Vegetables came from the garden or local farms, the fish from the rivers, lakes or the sea, and the meat usually came from the family pig.

00:17:38.537 --> 00:17:46.079
Although lamb is a popular meat in Wales today, in the past it was only eaten on special days or holidays.

00:17:46.139 --> 00:17:49.420
Historically there were no written recipes.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:55.414
Recipes were passed down orally from generation to generation.

00:17:55.474 --> 00:18:02.820
Cawl is a traditional Welsh soup, and is often considered the National Dish of Wales.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:07.262
It's a winter dish that originated in the 14th century.

00:18:07.322 --> 00:18:13.986
Once, it was made with bacon or beef, together with vegetables like carrots or potatoes.

00:18:14.046 --> 00:18:18.768
Today, Cawl is often made with lamb and leek.

00:18:18.828 --> 00:18:29.882
Laverbread is a traditional Welsh dish made from laver, an edible seaweed found on the rocks along the coastline of Wales.

00:18:29.942 --> 00:18:35.257
It's extremely nutritious because it's rich in minerals, proteins and vitamins.

00:18:35.317 --> 00:18:41.366
The laver is cooked for hours and then made into a greenish black puree or paste.

00:18:41.426 --> 00:18:48.541
A traditional Welsh breakfast usually consists of laverbread, bacon, sausage, eggs and cockles.

00:18:48.601 --> 00:18:52.603
Some people use laver as sauce or make it into soup.

00:18:52.663 --> 00:18:58.806
During the 18th century, laver was a basic and important food for the miners.

00:18:58.866 --> 00:19:07.216
The miners' wives collected it from the rocks on the coast and either sold it in the market or prepared laverbread with it.

00:19:07.276 --> 00:19:14.416
A typical miner started his day with laverbread as part of his traditional well-at-breakfast.

00:19:14.476 --> 00:19:23.884
Although the production of laver has become industrialized, the traditional methods of preparing it still remain in some parts of Wales today.

00:19:23.944 --> 00:19:32.423
Glamorgan Sausage The Glamorgan sausage is different from regular sausages because it's completely vegetarian.

00:19:32.484 --> 00:19:39.610
It's traditionally made with a white Welsh cheese called Caerphilly, leek and breadcrumbs.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:47.024
These sausages were particularly popular during World War II, when it was hard to find meat to buy.

00:19:47.084 --> 00:19:53.858
Bara Brith Barra means bread, and brief means speckled.

00:19:53.918 --> 00:19:58.188
So bara brith literally means speckled bread.

00:19:58.248 --> 00:20:01.938
It's a cake with dried fruit and spices.

00:20:01.998 --> 00:20:05.420
Bara brith is served with butter at tea time.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:11.622
In fact, in Argentina, there's a kind of bara brief called torta negra.

00:20:11.683 --> 00:20:23.750
This comes from the Welsh settlers who brought their traditional dishes with them when they arrived in Patagonia, and it still served today in Welsh tea houses in that area.

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:29.152
Drinks Wales produces beer, whiskey and wine.

00:20:29.212 --> 00:20:34.266
A popular new kind of gin called seaweed gin is made in Wales.

00:20:34.326 --> 00:20:40.320
It's made with seaweed from the Welsh coast and is processed at a local distillery.

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:47.376
The colour and taste of the seaweed depends on the season and this influences the taste of the gin.

00:20:47.436 --> 00:20:54.686
The seaweed makes the gin a bit salty but it's got a very distinct and unique taste.

00:20:54.746 --> 00:21:06.619
Chapter 5: Festivals and Traditions. St. David's Day. The most famous national holiday in Wales is St. David's Day.

00:21:06.679 --> 00:21:11.232
On the 1st of March, there are lots of celebrations on this day.

00:21:11.292 --> 00:21:15.336
Traditional songs are sung and traditional food is eaten.

00:21:15.396 --> 00:21:21.400
Some children dress in the Welsh national costume and hold leeks and daffodils too.

00:21:21.461 --> 00:21:24.600
St David is the patron saint of Wales.

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:30.634
He was born about 1500 years ago and he was educated at a monastery.

00:21:30.694 --> 00:21:33.125
Later he became a monk.

00:21:33.186 --> 00:21:38.280
According to legend, St David performed many miracles in his life.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:46.897
People believe that he helped a blind man to see again and brought a child back to life by wetting him with his tears.

00:21:46.957 --> 00:21:57.705
However, according to legend, his most famous miracle was when he was giving a sermon to a large number of people, and many couldn't see or hear him.

00:21:57.765 --> 00:22:06.528
Then a white dove landed on David's shoulder and the ground that David was standing on rose up to a large hill.

00:22:06.588 --> 00:22:09.840
Suddenly everybody could see and hear him.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:18.618
A church was built on this hill with pictures and stained glass windows showing St David with the white dove on his shoulder.

00:22:18.678 --> 00:22:23.163
To this day the white dove is the emblem of St David.

00:22:23.223 --> 00:22:28.594
There are many churches and chapels in St David's name, as well as a flag.

00:22:28.654 --> 00:22:35.736
The name Dafydd is the Welsh name for David, and many boys in Wales are called that.

00:22:35.796 --> 00:22:46.968
St. Dwynwen’s Day On the 25th of January, the Welsh celebrate St. Dwynwen’s Day which is a romantic day for lovers.

00:22:47.028 --> 00:22:52.110
People exchange cards, and there are celebrations all over Wales.

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:55.682
Dwynwen is the Welsh patron saint of lovers.

00:22:55.742 --> 00:23:02.735
In the 5th century, there was a king called Brychan Brycheiniog, who had 24 daughters.

00:23:02.795 --> 00:23:05.496
Dwynwen was the most beautiful.

00:23:05.556 --> 00:23:11.923
She was in love with a man called Maelon, but her father had a different man for her to marry.

00:23:11.983 --> 00:23:17.240
Dwynwen was sad and asked God to help her forget Maelon.

00:23:17.300 --> 00:23:23.566
That night, an angel visited Dwynwen in her sleep and gave her a sweet potion.

00:23:23.626 --> 00:23:29.510
This helped her forget her feelings for Maelon and turned him into a block of ice.

00:23:29.570 --> 00:23:32.502
God then gave the wind when three wishes.

00:23:32.562 --> 00:23:36.034
Her first wish was for Maelon to be thought.

00:23:36.094 --> 00:23:39.617
A second wish was for God to help all lovers.

00:23:39.677 --> 00:23:43.229
And her final wish was that she would never marry.

00:23:43.289 --> 00:23:45.910
God answered all three wishes.

00:23:45.971 --> 00:23:51.024
In return, Dwynwen became a nun and dedicated her life to God.

00:23:51.084 --> 00:23:58.137
She built the church of St. Dwynwen and founded a convent on the island of Llanddwyn near Anglesey.

00:23:58.198 --> 00:24:02.740
The remains of the church and Dwynwen's well can be seen on the island.

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:07.963
The well is still a favorite place for young Welsh lovers even today.

00:24:08.023 --> 00:24:17.968
Eisteddfod, the Eisteddfod, meaning sitting together or gathering, is a Welsh festival of music, literature and performance.

00:24:18.029 --> 00:24:22.311
The first Eisteddfod took place in the 12th century.

00:24:22.371 --> 00:24:32.016
Poets and musicians from all over Wales were invited to recite their poetry or play their music, and judges chose the best one.

00:24:32.077 --> 00:24:36.784
An elegant, high-backed chair with arms was awarded to the winner.

00:24:36.844 --> 00:24:46.794
This was a prestigious prize, because from the medieval period until the 1700s, ordinary people sat on simple stools.

00:24:46.854 --> 00:24:52.296
High-backed chairs were kept for royalty, and people of high status only.

00:24:52.356 --> 00:25:02.121
Today, the National Eisteddfod of Wales takes place every August for eight days, and it's the largest festival of poetry and music in Europe.

00:25:02.181 --> 00:25:05.786
The entire competition is held in Welsh.

00:25:05.846 --> 00:25:17.118
Chapter 6: Language. I met Owen later that evening, and we decided to tour together over the following days.

00:25:17.178 --> 00:25:20.622
I know all about Wales now, I told him.

00:25:20.682 --> 00:25:25.867
I spent an hour googling all sorts of interesting things about your country.

00:25:25.928 --> 00:25:30.880
Next time I go to the UK, I'll include Wales in the trip.

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:33.649
Good idea, Ben," said Owen.

00:25:33.709 --> 00:25:36.470
I'll show you lots of cool places.

00:25:36.529 --> 00:25:38.430
But I'm not speaking Welsh.

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:40.230
It's such a hard language.

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:43.131
You said a lot of people speak Welsh today, Owen.

00:25:43.191 --> 00:25:46.032
But what do you usually speak at home, I asked?

00:25:46.092 --> 00:25:47.762
Welsh or English?

00:25:47.822 --> 00:25:50.693
We speak Welsh most of the time?"

00:25:50.753 --> 00:25:52.034
Answered Owen.

00:25:52.094 --> 00:25:56.147
I speak it at work with my friends and with my family.

00:25:56.208 --> 00:25:58.741
Really, I said, surprised.

00:25:58.801 --> 00:26:04.052
Before I met you, I thought Welsh was a dead language, like Latin.

00:26:04.112 --> 00:26:06.003
Absolutely not.

00:26:06.063 --> 00:26:08.724
Most people can speak and write Welsh.

00:26:08.784 --> 00:26:13.195
It's an obligatory subject in all schools until the age of 16.

00:26:13.255 --> 00:26:17.366
Children learn it as either a first or second language.

00:26:17.426 --> 00:26:22.186
In fact, quite a lot of children are taught all their subjects in Welsh.

00:26:22.246 --> 00:26:26.466
When you come to Wales, you will see and hear Welsh everywhere.

00:26:26.526 --> 00:26:31.600
There are radio stations and a TV channel with all their programs in Welsh.

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:38.184
Also, there are Welsh newspapers and magazines, and the road signs are in both languages.

00:26:38.244 --> 00:26:41.476
Welsh is really Britain's oldest language.

00:26:41.536 --> 00:26:47.409
The Romans were the first people to write in Welsh, and there are elements of Latin in the language.

00:26:47.469 --> 00:26:57.030
From the 7th century, the people of Wales were called Cymry, which means Welsh, and Wales created its own laws in Welsh.

00:26:57.090 --> 00:27:00.559
But the Welsh language has also had its battles.

00:27:00.619 --> 00:27:09.908
In 1536, Henry VIII passed a law which prohibited using Welsh in the legal system and in administrative affairs.

00:27:09.968 --> 00:27:20.031
Then in 1549, the English Parliament passed the Act of Uniformity which said all public prayer had to be in English.

00:27:20.091 --> 00:27:32.026
Fortunately for us, in 1563, Queen Elizabeth I allowed the Bible and the prayer book to be printed in Welsh, and this brought the language back again.

00:27:32.086 --> 00:27:38.034
Welsh people went to church regularly, so the translations helped the language survive.

00:27:38.094 --> 00:27:44.633
Welsh literature, especially poetry, has helped to preserve the language and national identity.

00:27:44.693 --> 00:27:55.106
In 1870, the English Education Act made English the official language of instruction at schools to put an end to the Welsh language.

00:27:55.166 --> 00:27:58.199
Children who spoke Welsh were punished.

00:27:58.259 --> 00:28:05.534
If a teacher heard a child speaking Welsh, he would put a Welsh Not sign around the child's neck.

00:28:05.594 --> 00:28:09.026
Even the names of towns were changed into English.

00:28:09.086 --> 00:28:13.519
For example, Caerdydd became Cardiff.

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:17.322
So, how has the language survived until now Owen?

00:28:17.382 --> 00:28:18.474
I asked.

00:28:18.534 --> 00:28:23.340
Well, a sense of national identity has led to the revival of the language.

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:31.458
Now there's Welsh language instruction in schools, education centers, and on Welsh radio stations and TV channels.

00:28:31.519 --> 00:28:35.122
Ben, have you heard of Patagonia?

00:28:35.182 --> 00:28:37.414
That's in South America, isn't it?

00:28:37.474 --> 00:28:38.458
I asked.

00:28:38.518 --> 00:28:42.282
Yes, in Argentina, Owen answered.

00:28:42.342 --> 00:28:47.906
In 1865, several Welsh people arrived in Patagonia and stayed there.

00:28:47.966 --> 00:28:52.298
They wanted to keep their culture and language, so they spoke Welsh.

00:28:52.358 --> 00:28:58.772
Then more Welsh people started to visit the region in the 60s, and this revived the language.

00:28:58.832 --> 00:29:03.745
Today they still speak Patagonian Welsh there, a dialect of Welsh.

00:29:03.805 --> 00:29:08.996
Welsh teachers are sent to Patagonia to instruct local teachers in the language.

00:29:09.056 --> 00:29:14.138
Apparently speaking Welsh is a sign of prestige in Patagonia.

00:29:14.198 --> 00:29:16.859
Can you understand Patagonian Welsh?

00:29:16.919 --> 00:29:17.899
I asked.

00:29:17.959 --> 00:29:19.600
Oh yes, said Owen.

00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:22.130
It's not so different from our Welsh.

00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:28.962
Owen lifted his cup of coffee, looked at me and said, Cymru am byth.

00:29:29.022 --> 00:29:32.114
I tried unsuccessfully to repeat that.

00:29:32.174 --> 00:29:34.555
What does it mean, I asked?

00:29:34.615 --> 00:29:40.106
"Wales forever," smiled Owen, and drank some more coffee.

00:29:40.166 --> 00:29:41.797
Chapter 7.

00:29:41.857 --> 00:29:43.278
Sport.

00:29:43.338 --> 00:29:45.248
Rugby.

00:29:45.308 --> 00:29:53.541
Wales participates in many world-sporting events, like the Rugby World Cup and the FIFA World Cup.

00:29:53.601 --> 00:30:00.614
Although the Welsh like playing football, the sport that most people associate with Wales is rugby.

00:30:00.674 --> 00:30:06.697
Rugby is considered the national sport and it's played and followed throughout Wales.

00:30:06.757 --> 00:30:16.571
The Welsh Rugby Union, WRU, supervises the different rugby clubs, the national team, the referees, players and trainers.

00:30:16.631 --> 00:30:18.981
Rugby isn't just for men.

00:30:19.041 --> 00:30:22.242
There's a WRU team for women too.

00:30:22.302 --> 00:30:34.008
Both teams have got the same emblem, the Prince of Wales' feathers, and they both wear the same uniform, red shirts, white shorts, and red socks.

00:30:34.068 --> 00:30:39.050
At every game, the enthusiastic fans sing Welsh rugby songs.

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:42.022
These are often folk songs or hymns.

00:30:42.082 --> 00:30:46.715
In fact, some of these religious songs were sung in the late 19th century.

00:30:46.775 --> 00:30:59.496
The oldest Welsh rugby song is Men of Harlech, which is about Welsh resistance during the siege of Harlech Castle between 1461 and 1468.

00:30:59.556 --> 00:31:15.074
The Millennium Stadium The Millennium Stadium, recently renamed the Principality Stadium, opened in June 1999 and it's the biggest arena in Wales.

00:31:15.134 --> 00:31:20.894
It's got a retractable roof and it can seat 74,500 visitors.

00:31:20.954 --> 00:31:33.704
At international rugby games, the Welsh fans stand in the stadium, all loudly saying their national anthem, Land of My Fathers, Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau.

00:31:33.764 --> 00:31:47.444
Besides the National Rugby Union team, the stadium also hosts the Welsh national football team and has had music concerts there, including Madonna, U2 and Red Hot Chili Peppers.

00:31:47.504 --> 00:31:54.392
Jousting In medieval times, the most popular Welsh sport was jousting.

00:31:54.452 --> 00:32:02.980
Wales is a country of castles, and jousting was once the favourite form of entertainment of kings, knights and noblemen.

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:12.848
Two knights fought on horseback, and the objective was to break the lance on his opponent's armour or shield and to make him fall from his horse.

00:32:12.908 --> 00:32:16.012
It was a battle of speed and courage.

00:32:16.072 --> 00:32:24.188
In medieval times, the winning jouster was given a prize like a gold ring, a sword or an elaborate helmet.

00:32:24.248 --> 00:32:26.842
But the loser often died.

00:32:26.902 --> 00:32:29.874
Jousting was a dangerous sport.

00:32:29.934 --> 00:32:34.304
Every year in June, Cardiff Castle has a medieval weekend.

00:32:34.364 --> 00:32:37.221
With traditional jousting for the visitors.

00:32:37.281 --> 00:32:41.054
Even today there are riding schools that teach jousting.

00:32:41.114 --> 00:32:48.710
The sport requires control and physical strength because the armour is made of steel which makes it very heavy.

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:58.968
Jousters must train daily and must also be good at horse riding and have good balance because they need to hold a long lance in one hand while riding.

00:32:59.028 --> 00:33:01.700
That's not so easy at all.

00:33:01.760 --> 00:33:05.702
Chapter 8: Famous Figures.

00:33:05.762 --> 00:33:12.994
Owain Glyndŵr One of the greatest Welsh heroes was Owain Glyndŵr.

00:33:13.054 --> 00:33:17.875
He was the last native Welshman to have the title Prince of Wales.

00:33:17.935 --> 00:33:20.586
Owen was a strong charismatic leader.

00:33:20.646 --> 00:33:25.278
He united and led the Welsh against the English in the 14th century.

00:33:25.338 --> 00:33:33.766
Welshmen from all social classes joined Owen in his revolt and supported his desire for Welsh independence from England.

00:33:33.826 --> 00:33:41.352
After years of fighting, the revolt ended and by 1410 Owen was a wanted fugitive.

00:33:41.412 --> 00:33:51.881
No one knows exactly when he died, but his memory remains strong throughout Wales and many parks and streets have his name.

00:33:51.941 --> 00:34:02.208
Dylan Thomas Popular Welsh poet Dylan Thomas is probably one of the most important poets of the 20th century.

00:34:02.268 --> 00:34:08.092
He was born in Swansea on the southern coast of Wales in 1914.

00:34:08.153 --> 00:34:17.331
His father and English literature professor used to recite Shakespeare to Thomas, inspiring a love of literature from an early age.

00:34:17.391 --> 00:34:21.944
Much of his inspiration and ideas came from the Welsh countryside.

00:34:22.004 --> 00:34:25.536
He is famous for his collected poems of Dylan Thomas.

00:34:25.596 --> 00:34:32.279
Radio plays like Under Milk Wood and a book, Portrait of the Artist as a Young Dog.

00:34:32.339 --> 00:34:38.912
Surprisingly, Dylan Thomas, the most famous Welsh poet, never wrote in Welsh.

00:34:38.972 --> 00:34:43.611
Dylan Thomas died in the United States at 39 years of age.

00:34:43.671 --> 00:34:49.760
And there is a plaque to his memory in Poets Corner in Westminster Abbey, London.

00:34:49.821 --> 00:34:56.766
Welsh celebrities, name, birthplace, profession, achievements.

00:34:56.826 --> 00:35:07.522
Gareth Bale 1989 Cardiff Top footballer, plays for Real Madrid and the Welsh national team.

00:35:07.582 --> 00:35:09.944
Sir Anthony Hopkins, 1937.

00:35:09.984 --> 00:35:12.538
Port Talbot, actor.

00:35:12.598 --> 00:35:32.679
Works in cinema, theatre and television, one Oscar for Best Actor in the Silence of the Lambs, 1991 and Two Emmys, knighted by Queen Elizabeth II in 1993, received a star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame.

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:36.625
Sir Tom Jones, 1940.

00:35:36.685 --> 00:35:38.356
Pontypridd.

00:35:38.416 --> 00:35:39.558
Singer.

00:35:39.618 --> 00:35:50.118
Popular Singer, a solo of 100 million records has 36 top 40 hits in the UK and 19 in the United States.

00:35:50.178 --> 00:35:58.521
Received a Grammy Award and two Brit Awards, knighted by Queen Elizabeth II for services to music.

00:35:58.581 --> 00:36:26.656
Roald Dahl 1916-1990 Cardiff Writer, poet and screenwriter His most famous works include Charlie and the Chocolate Factory, Matilda, The Witches, Fantastic Mr. Fox and James and the Giant Peach sold more than 250 million books worldwide and received several awards for his contribution to literature.

00:36:26.716 --> 00:36:45.181
Catherine Zeta-Jones 1969 Swansea Actress Works in theatre, cinema and television has won several awards including an Oscar for Chicago and a CBE for her film and humanitarian works.

00:36:45.241 --> 00:36:51.025
She appeared in Intolerable Cruelty, The Terminal and The Legend of Zorro.

00:36:51.086 --> 00:36:56.551
Chapter 9: Welsh Symbols. The Flag.

00:36:56.611 --> 00:37:06.010
The Welsh flag has got two large horizontal stripes, one green and one white, and a large red dragon in the middle.

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:12.906
Green and white were the colours of the 15th century Tudor royal family who came from Wales.

00:37:12.966 --> 00:37:18.784
They added the red dragon to the Tudor flag to emphasize their Welsh descent.

00:37:18.844 --> 00:37:26.920
However, it was only in 1959 that the flag officially became the national flag of Wales.

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:31.261
The Red Dragon supposedly comes from the time of King Arthur.

00:37:31.321 --> 00:37:44.834
According to legend, the wizard Merlin had a vision of a Red Dragon, the native Britons, fighting a White Dragon, the Saxon invaders, and the Red Dragon won the battle.

00:37:44.894 --> 00:37:50.610
Today, the Red Dragon represents all things that are connected to Wales.

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:52.231
The Leek.

00:37:52.291 --> 00:37:57.886
It might surprise you to read that one of the national emblems of Wales is the Leek.

00:37:57.946 --> 00:38:07.519
According to one legend, King Kadwalader ordered his soldiers to wear a Leek on their helmets when they were fighting the Saxons in a Leek field.

00:38:07.579 --> 00:38:13.076
The leeks helped them identify themselves and gave them good camouflage from their enemy.

00:38:13.136 --> 00:38:18.820
Another legend says that St David always ate leeks, even when he was fasting.

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:21.502
So they became his personal symbol.

00:38:21.562 --> 00:38:29.558
Today many Welsh people wear a leek on St David's day to celebrate their victory against the Saxons.

00:38:29.618 --> 00:38:38.100
The Daffodil The daffodil is another symbol of Wales, but that may be due to some language confusion.

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:47.144
A leek in Welsh is kenhinen, and a daffodil in Welsh is kenhinen peder, which means Peter's leek.

00:38:47.205 --> 00:38:56.049
Daffodils are spring flowers, and since St David's day is on the 1st of March, many people wear a daffodil on that day too.

00:38:56.109 --> 00:38:58.580
Traditional Welsh costume.

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:01.984
Welsh women had a traditional costume.

00:39:02.044 --> 00:39:06.960
But it was mainly poorer rural women who wore it at the market.

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:16.018
There are many different variations of this costume, but it generally consists of a striped under dress with a shawl and an apron.

00:39:16.078 --> 00:39:21.722
And the most important part of the Welsh costume is the tall chimney hat.

00:39:21.782 --> 00:39:32.362
The Welsh costume became a symbol of national identity, and today women wear it on special occasions, like St David's Day or at Eisteddfod.

00:39:32.422 --> 00:39:40.688
Love Spoons You can't visit a Welsh gift shop without seeing a variety of love spoons.

00:39:40.748 --> 00:39:44.320
The Welsh love spoon is a very popular souvenir.

00:39:44.380 --> 00:39:58.234
Made by hand from one piece of wood, it is carved with a special message or symbol, for example, a heart for love, a horseshoe for luck, a cross for religion and bells for marriage.

00:39:58.294 --> 00:40:05.514
The tradition of carving and giving a love spoon to a girl originated in Wales in the 17th century.

00:40:05.574 --> 00:40:11.218
The spoons were not actually used, they were displayed as ornaments on the wall.

00:40:11.278 --> 00:40:15.492
Young men carved their love and intentions on a love spoon.

00:40:15.552 --> 00:40:20.605
If a girl kept the love spoon, it was a sign that she was interested in him.

00:40:20.665 --> 00:40:24.864
If she returned it, it was clear she didn't want the man.

00:40:24.924 --> 00:40:42.944
The love-spoon tradition disappeared in the 19th century, but over the last forty years there's been a revival and people, especially tourists, by hand-made love-spoons for their loved ones on special occasions, like anniversaries, births or graduations.

00:40:43.004 --> 00:40:48.088
It's certainly more original than a box of chocolates or flowers.

00:40:48.149 --> 00:40:49.960
Chapter 10.

00:40:50.020 --> 00:41:01.058
Myths and Legends. It is not surprising that a country with so many castles has lots of legends and ghost stories.

00:41:01.118 --> 00:41:06.181
There are plenty of stories of haunted castles and buildings in Wales.

00:41:06.242 --> 00:41:12.966
Strange things seem to happen in Cardiff Castle, particularly in its southwest corner.

00:41:13.026 --> 00:41:24.312
Apparently, at 3.45 every morning, the heavy doors of the dining room open and closed by themselves, and the lights go on and off.

00:41:24.372 --> 00:41:30.054
Another scary story is the sudden appearance of the second Marquis of Bute.

00:41:30.114 --> 00:41:33.896
He lived in Cardiff Castle in the 19th century.

00:41:33.956 --> 00:41:40.430
According to some people, he sometimes appears in the library, dressed in a long red coat.

00:41:40.490 --> 00:41:43.772
That's enough to terrify the tourists.

00:41:43.832 --> 00:41:52.808
Another castle with a reputation for ghosts is Castell Coch, Red Castle, which is nine kilometers from Cardiff.

00:41:52.868 --> 00:41:58.660
It looks beautiful and romantic, but it's got dark dungeons and corridors.

00:41:58.721 --> 00:42:02.952
In fact, on Halloween, there are special ghost tours there.

00:42:03.013 --> 00:42:10.416
There's a legend about the Castell Coch ghost, who continues to search for the treasure he hid in the walls of the castle.

00:42:10.476 --> 00:42:16.699
Some people say they experience a strange feeling in the chapel, as if someone is watching them.

00:42:16.759 --> 00:42:23.368
Some hear strange sounds in the great hall, and others maintain that a spirit touched them.

00:42:23.428 --> 00:42:30.748
If you're a nervous person, it's probably best not to take the Castell Coch Night Tour.

00:42:30.808 --> 00:42:35.690
Devil's Bridge, a village in West Wales.

00:42:35.750 --> 00:42:44.256
Another popular tourist site is a village called Pontarfynach, which means the bridge on the Manach.

00:42:44.316 --> 00:42:51.262
This unusual bridge is actually not one bridge, but three bridges, one built on the other.

00:42:51.322 --> 00:43:00.830
The first bridge was built of stone in the 11th century, the second in 1753 and the third Iron Bridge in 1901.

00:43:00.890 --> 00:43:10.006
Visitors can walk down stone steps called Jacob's Ladder from the third bridge to the first bridge to see a beautiful waterfall.

00:43:10.066 --> 00:43:13.558
So how did this interesting feature get its name?

00:43:13.618 --> 00:43:15.480
Devil's Bridge.

00:43:15.540 --> 00:43:20.112
According to legend, the bridge was built by the Devil.

00:43:20.172 --> 00:43:27.454
Apparently, in the 11th century, the devil visited Wales and met an old woman with her dog.

00:43:27.514 --> 00:43:33.636
The old woman was worried about her cow, which was trapped on the other side of the river.

00:43:33.696 --> 00:43:44.659
The devil wanted the woman's soul, so he agreed to build a bridge across the river in return for the soul of the first living thing that crossed the bridge.

00:43:44.719 --> 00:43:47.477
But the old woman was clever.

00:43:47.537 --> 00:43:53.255
As soon as the devil finished building the bridge, she threw some bread onto the bridge.

00:43:53.315 --> 00:43:59.146
Her dog ran after the bread and became the first living thing to cross the bridge.

00:43:59.206 --> 00:44:05.887
The devil was so humiliated by this that he left Wales and never returned again.

00:44:05.947 --> 00:44:11.938
King Arthur There are many legends of King Arthur and Wales.

00:44:11.998 --> 00:44:16.755
Two lakes in Snowdonia, Llyn Llydaw and Lake Ogwen.

00:44:16.815 --> 00:44:22.204
Are both reputed to contain King Arthur's magical sword Excalibur.

00:44:22.264 --> 00:44:27.596
There are also rocks that supposedly show the hoofprints of King Arthur's horse.

00:44:27.656 --> 00:44:38.816
A stone on the banks of Llyn Barfog has strange hoofprints, supposedly from the time when King Arthur and his horse pulled a monster from the lake.

00:44:38.876 --> 00:44:44.252
There are lots of fantasy tales and legends about the western coast of Wales.

00:44:44.312 --> 00:44:52.554
These remote places inspire frightening thoughts and wild imagination which make the best ghost stories.

00:44:52.614 --> 00:45:06.700
Chapter 11: Arts. Wales, Land of Song. Wales is known as the Land of Song and it has many Welsh male choirs.

00:45:06.761 --> 00:45:16.207
These originated in the 19th century during the Industrial Revolution and are an important part of Welsh culture and identity.

00:45:16.267 --> 00:45:21.289
Today there are over 100 male choirs in Wales.

00:45:21.349 --> 00:45:28.330
The industrial workforce in Wales came from the working class and most of the workers were men.

00:45:28.390 --> 00:45:37.536
They worked long hours in the ports, mines or railways, and they often organized themselves into a local choir within their workplace.

00:45:37.596 --> 00:45:41.920
The choir was a way for them to relax after a hard day's work.

00:45:41.980 --> 00:45:54.808
It gave these hard-working men a sense of identity and pride, and a unique chance to see other places in Wales and England, because choirs often competed against each other.

00:45:54.868 --> 00:46:03.108
The choir sang mainly traditional Welsh songs and hymns, but sometimes light opera or popular modern songs.

00:46:03.168 --> 00:46:09.982
The songs were often accompanied by the triple harp, a harp with three rows of strings.

00:46:10.042 --> 00:46:15.914
This instrument was played so much in Wales that it became known as the Welsh harp.

00:46:15.974 --> 00:46:24.564
Today it's considered the natural instrument of Wales, and traditional Welsh folk music is often played on this harp.

00:46:24.624 --> 00:46:27.381
My grandfather was in a choir.

00:46:27.401 --> 00:46:29.481
Oh, until Ben.

00:46:29.541 --> 00:46:38.594
He told me that the first time he left his village was when he was 23, when his choir sang in a competition in Cardiff.

00:46:38.654 --> 00:46:42.155
He used to tell me stories of his life in the village.

00:46:42.215 --> 00:46:47.446
He said that when he was a boy, choirs were a natural part of village life.

00:46:47.506 --> 00:46:53.867
I can still hear his voice saying, When I was a lad, you did what your father did.

00:46:53.927 --> 00:46:58.960
You left school, got a job in the mines, and became a member of a choir.

00:46:59.020 --> 00:47:01.712
That's how it was in the village.

00:47:01.772 --> 00:47:12.580
Wales in films and TV The Welsh countryside is a great location for films, because it has an interesting and diverse landscape.

00:47:12.640 --> 00:47:17.744
Beautiful long coasts, high mountains and magnificent cliffs.

00:47:17.804 --> 00:47:26.208
In Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows, there is a scene when Harry and his friends hide in Dobby's Shell Cottage.

00:47:26.268 --> 00:47:33.120
This cottage was built especially for the film and the sandy beach of Pembrokeshire, West Wales.

00:47:33.180 --> 00:47:42.010
Although the cottage isn't there anymore, you can still walk along the beach and imagine you are walking with Harry, Hermione and Ron.

00:47:42.071 --> 00:47:48.224
In 2010, this same location was chosen for the film Robin Hood.

00:47:48.284 --> 00:47:52.828
This time, it was used to present a ferocious battle.

00:47:52.888 --> 00:47:57.982
Horsemen dressed in armour and with flags in their hands galloped along the beach.

00:47:58.042 --> 00:48:09.324
The main actor, Russell Crowe, liked the location so much that he camped on the sand for several nights instead of flying back to his luxurious hotel in London.

00:48:09.384 --> 00:48:22.555
American actress Kristen Stewart was also fascinated by the beautiful Pembrokeshire Sans when she played Snow White in the film Snow White and the Huntsman 2012.

00:48:22.615 --> 00:48:33.092
There is an exciting scene in which Snow White rides with 80 men on horseback and this was filmed along Marloes Sands in Pembrokeshire.

00:48:33.152 --> 00:48:44.018
Doctor Who is a British science fiction television series which began in 1963 and continued until 1989.

00:48:44.078 --> 00:48:49.271
It was later revived in 2005 due to popular demand.

00:48:49.331 --> 00:48:56.726
The series is about a Doctor who travels in time in his special time travel machine, the TARDIS.

00:48:56.786 --> 00:49:00.158
The series is filmed in South Wales.

00:49:00.218 --> 00:49:08.932
For tourists who like science fiction, filming and technology, a visit to the Doctor Who experience in Cardiff Bay is a must.

00:49:08.992 --> 00:49:19.947
There they can learn about the filming of Doctor Who and see many of the different props like the TARDIS, the monsters and the costumes that appear in the series.

00:49:20.008 --> 00:49:24.780
They can also discover how they create the amazing special effects.

00:49:24.840 --> 00:49:28.840
It's a very interesting and popular attraction.

00:49:28.900 --> 00:49:40.616
Chapter 12: Wales Today. Tourism. In the past, coal and slate mining were the main industries in Wales.

00:49:40.676 --> 00:49:47.610
But today the main industry is tourism, with over 8 million visitors a year.

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:53.120
Most of the tourists come from other parts of the United Kingdom, especially England.

00:49:53.180 --> 00:50:00.637
Wales also attracts tourists from the United States and Europe, particularly France and Germany.

00:50:00.697 --> 00:50:12.852
Visiting the numerous castles and other historic sites is a popular activity for tourists, especially in the summer, when you can watch representations of battles and jousting.

00:50:12.912 --> 00:50:17.864
Wales is a wonderful country to visit if you like the outdoor life.

00:50:17.924 --> 00:50:25.757
The most popular tourist activities are climbing in the mountains and other sports such as canoeing and cycling.

00:50:25.818 --> 00:50:32.730
But if you like more of a challenge, there's also hang gliding or downhill mountain biking.

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:43.661
There are over 1 million Americans of Welsh descent, so many of them come to discover their origins and learn about their ancestors, culture and traditions.

00:50:43.722 --> 00:50:49.573
Wales also has got some beautiful and unusual glamping sights.

00:50:49.633 --> 00:50:53.165
Glamping means glamorous camping.

00:50:53.225 --> 00:51:02.741
Not everyone enjoys the normal camping experience, sleeping bags, tents, fires or worrying about keeping their things dry when it rains.

00:51:02.801 --> 00:51:12.892
When you go glamping, you can enjoy the positive experiences of being outdoors but with every comfort like beds, bathrooms and heating.

00:51:12.952 --> 00:51:19.189
There are all kinds of Welsh glamping sites, tree houses, cabins and tents.

00:51:19.249 --> 00:51:24.880
Some are more luxurious than others, but they're all very comfortable.

00:51:24.940 --> 00:51:26.842
Fashion.

00:51:26.902 --> 00:51:36.274
Wales has got many sheep, so it's not surprising that wool has played an important part in Welsh industry throughout the years.

00:51:36.334 --> 00:51:41.724
In fact, spinning and weaving of wool already exists in prehistoric times.

00:51:41.784 --> 00:51:47.797
The production of wooling Wales was a very important industry in the country until World War I.

00:51:47.857 --> 00:51:54.422
Today there are only a few wool mills and these are usually operated by small families.

00:51:54.482 --> 00:51:59.466
They still produce traditional Welsh cloth using old looms.

00:51:59.526 --> 00:52:10.522
Protecting the Environment Like other countries, Wales is looking for ways to protect the environment and reduce carbon dioxide emissions.

00:52:10.582 --> 00:52:15.614
One solution is building a wind farm to produce electricity.

00:52:15.674 --> 00:52:21.198
Wind power doesn't consume fuel and it doesn't generate air pollution.

00:52:21.258 --> 00:52:25.721
Gwynt y Môr is an example of an offshore wind farm.

00:52:25.782 --> 00:52:32.356
It's off the coast of North Wales and it's the second largest offshore wind farm in the world.

00:52:32.417 --> 00:52:40.234
It has 160 wind turbines that can produce enough energy for approximately 30% of homes in Wales.

00:52:40.294 --> 00:52:51.666
Welsh independence Some Welsh people prefer to maintain their national identity without leaving the United Kingdom.

00:52:51.726 --> 00:52:59.822
But there are other Welsh people who want independence, and certainly more control over decisions that affect their lives.

00:52:59.882 --> 00:53:05.210
There are political parties who want Welsh independence from the United Kingdom.

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:13.088
One of the main parties that promotes this ideology is the Welsh nationalist party, Plaid Cymru.

00:53:13.148 --> 00:53:23.404
In the early 20th century, there was a fear among many Welsh people that their language and culture would disappear as English became the official language.

00:53:23.464 --> 00:53:28.578
As a result, Plaid Cymru was founded in 1925.

00:53:28.638 --> 00:53:44.707
One of its main objectives was to create a bilingual society by reviving the Welsh language It also wanted to promote Wales' contribution in the global market, as well as to become a member of the United Nations.

00:53:44.767 --> 00:53:53.159
The recent vote for Great Britain to exit from the European Union, Brexit, has raised the question of Welsh independence.

00:53:53.219 --> 00:54:05.086
Most Welsh people voted to leave the European Union, and Plaid Cymru's leader sees Brexit as an opportunity for Wales to become an independent country.

00:54:05.146 --> 00:54:14.857
Many Welsh people may disagree about independence, but most of them want to promote Welsh identity and language throughout Wales today.

00:54:14.918 --> 00:54:26.438
The Welsh are proud of their country, their traditions and their contribution to all areas of British life.

00:54:26.498 --> 00:54:31.634
Swansea Jack.

00:54:31.694 --> 00:54:34.835
Can dogs help us in a time of crisis?

00:54:34.895 --> 00:54:40.257
The story of a black dog called Swansea Jack proves that they can.

00:54:40.318 --> 00:54:43.239
Jack was born in 1930.

00:54:43.299 --> 00:54:48.692
He lived with his master William Thomas in the coastal city of Swansea.

00:54:48.752 --> 00:54:52.383
He became famous for saving people from the sea.

00:54:52.443 --> 00:55:00.632
When Swansea Jack heard a cry of help, he dived into the cold water and pulled the person to a safe place.

00:55:00.692 --> 00:55:06.798
His first rescue was in June 1931 when he was only a year old.

00:55:06.858 --> 00:55:10.827
He jumped into the sea and saved a 12-year-old boy.

00:55:10.887 --> 00:55:16.485
Nobody knew what he had done until a few weeks later when he rescued another swimmer.

00:55:16.545 --> 00:55:23.418
This time, a crowd of people saw his actions and his photograph appeared in the local newspaper.

00:55:23.478 --> 00:55:27.256
Then, news of his first rescue became known.

00:55:27.316 --> 00:55:32.231
According to legend, he saved 27 people in his lifetime.

00:55:32.291 --> 00:55:45.423
In fact, Swansea Jack saved so many people that he won the bravest dog of the year award, a silver collar, a silver cup from the Lord Mayor of London and two bronze medals.

00:55:45.483 --> 00:55:54.914
Dog lovers enjoyed meeting Swansea Jack and he often appeared at carnivals and shows with his owner to help raise money for charity.

00:55:54.974 --> 00:56:01.286
Sadly, Swansea Jack died in 1937 after eating rat poison.

00:56:01.346 --> 00:56:13.744
The people of Swansea were so proud of their dog that they built a special monument to him, which visitors can see today near Swansea's rugby ground.

00:56:13.804 --> 00:56:19.858
When people go to agricultural shows, they usually see sheep dogs herding animals.

00:56:19.918 --> 00:56:28.730
But for several years the star of these shows in Wales and other places in the UK was a woolly white sheep called Rolo.

00:56:28.790 --> 00:56:34.591
Welsh farmer Emlyn Roberts became Rolo's owner in 2005.

00:56:34.651 --> 00:56:39.982
He rescued her on a visit to friends when she was a tiny lamb in Caernarfon.

00:56:40.042 --> 00:56:47.943
Everyone thought the lamb had died, he said, but then he saw she was still alive so he decided to take her home.

00:56:48.003 --> 00:56:57.316
The little lamb recovered and emlyn called her Rolo because her favourite treat was a famous British chocolate sweet called Rolo.

00:56:57.376 --> 00:57:06.559
Rolo grew up with emlyn's sheep dogs and it became obvious she thought she was also a dog because she started to behave like them.

00:57:06.619 --> 00:57:11.557
She ate her meals with them and sat at emlyn's feet while he watched TV.

00:57:11.617 --> 00:57:19.835
But Emlyn was even more surprised when she started to copy the dogs and bring his ducks together and into their pen for the night.

00:57:19.895 --> 00:57:27.366
In 2006, he started to take her to agricultural shows and amazed the crowds with her talents.

00:57:27.426 --> 00:57:33.966
Besides herding ducks around a special course, she jumped over fences and did other tricks too.

00:57:34.026 --> 00:57:37.820
Sadly, Rolo died in 2013.

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:43.878
But Emlyn is now training Rolo's nephew, Horlicks, to do tricks and dance.

00:57:43.938 --> 00:57:48.145
Emlyn hopes the people will love him the same way they loved Rolo.